V=[
X(1,'THE DUST OF THE GURU’S FEET',HS('Hanuman bowing at a teacher’s feet before singing','GURU FIRST','book',fold=True,mace=False),
 'Cleaning the mirror of my mind with the dust of my guru’s lotus feet, I sing the spotless glory of Rāma, best of the Raghus, which gives the four fruits of life.',
 [('śrīguru carana saroja raja','<b>dust of the holy guru’s lotus feet</b>'),('nija manu mukuru sudhāri','<b>polishing the mirror of my own mind</b>'),('baranaūṃ raghubara bimala jasu','<b>I describe the pure fame of Rāma</b>'),('jo dāyaku phala cāri','<b>which gives the four fruits</b>')],
 'This opening dohā is not Tulasīdāsa’s own composition for the Cālīsā: it is the very first dohā of the Ayodhyā Kāṇḍa of his Rāmacaritamānas. Tradition placed it here so that the hymn to Hanumān begins, as all his work does, with the guru and with Rāma. A dohā is a two-line couplet of 13 and 11 beats in each half; the Cālīsā’s forty verses are caupāīs, quatrains in a 16-beat metre.',
 'The image of the mind as a mirror is old in Indian thought: a dusty mirror shows nothing clearly, however bright the light. The surprise is the cleaning cloth: not effort or learning but the dust of the teacher’s feet, the humblest thing imaginable. Many read “guru” here as Hanumān himself, the teacher who leads the devotee to Rāma; others as one’s own human teacher. The four fruits are dharma, artha, kāma and mokṣa: right living, wealth, fulfilled desire and freedom.',
 'Before you start anything hard, clean the mirror: drop the noise and the pride, and look to the people who taught you.',
 'Clean the <b>mirror of the mind</b> first.'),
X(2,'GIVE ME STRENGTH, WISDOM, KNOWLEDGE',HS('Hanuman blessing a kneeling devotee','BALA · BUDDHI · VIDYĀ','light'),
 'Knowing this body to be without wisdom, I remember you, son of the Wind. Give me strength, wisdom and knowledge, and take away my troubles and my faults.',
 [('buddhihīna tanu jānike','<b>knowing myself to be without wisdom</b>'),('sumirauṃ pavana-kumāra','<b>I remember the Wind’s son</b>'),('bala budhi bidyā dehu mohiṃ','<b>give me strength, intellect, learning</b>'),('harahu kalesa bikāra','<b>remove suffering and faults</b>')],
 'This dohā turns from Rāma to Hanumān and states the request of the whole hymn. Together with the first dohā it forms the standard opening in printed editions such as Gītā Press. Many families begin chanting from this line alone.',
 'The confession comes before the request: “buddhihīna”, I am without understanding. Only one who admits a lack can receive. The three gifts are chosen with care: bala, strength of body; budhi, sharp judgement; bidyā, learning. Hanumān has all three in the Rāmāyaṇa, and Rāma himself praised his learning before his strength. Kalesa (kleśa) is pain, and in Yoga philosophy the five root afflictions; bikāra (vikāra) means distortions of the mind such as anger, greed and fear.',
 'Ask for all three: a strong body, a clear head and real knowledge. Any one without the others goes wrong.',
 '<b>Strength, wisdom, learning</b>: ask for all three.'),
X(3,'OCEAN OF WISDOM',HS('Hanuman standing above the three worlds, lighting them','JAYA HANUMĀNA','ocean'),
 'Victory to Hanumān, ocean of knowledge and virtue! Victory to the lord of the vānaras, who lights up the three worlds!',
 [('jaya hanumāna','<b>victory to Hanumān</b>'),('jñāna guna sāgara','<b>ocean of knowledge and virtues</b>'),('jaya kapīsa','<b>victory to the lord of the vānaras</b>'),('tihuṃ loka ujāgara','<b>illuminator of the three worlds</b>')],
 'The forty caupāīs begin here, and this is the line almost every Hindu child knows. “Cālīsā” simply means “a forty”, a hymn of forty verses; the form was common in North Indian devotion, and there are Cālīsās to Śiva, Durgā, Gaṇeśa and many others, most modelled on this one.',
 'The opening praises wisdom before strength, as Vālmīki did. Kapīśa, lord of the vānaras, is a title of honour: Sugrīva was king, but Hanumān is lord in the sense of the greatest. The three worlds are earth, sky and heaven, or in later reading the waking, dreaming and deep-sleep states. Ujāgara means one who makes things bright and famous: Hanumān’s deeds light up every world where they are told.',
 'Start with praise. Saying what is good in someone, even God, changes the one who says it.',
 '<b>Ocean of wisdom</b>, light of the three worlds.'),
X(4,'RĀMA’S MESSENGER',HS('Hanuman carrying a message, Rama behind him','RĀMADŪTA',rama=True),
 'You are Rāma’s messenger, home of strength beyond measure, known as Añjanā’s son and the son of the Wind.',
 [('rāmadūta','<b>Rāma’s messenger</b>'),('atulita bala dhāmā','<b>abode of incomparable strength</b>'),('añjani-putra','<b>son of Añjanā</b>'),('pavanasuta nāmā','<b>named son of the Wind</b>')],
 'Rāmadūta is the title Hanumān gives himself in Laṅkā: when Rāvaṇa asks who he is, he answers “I am the messenger of Rāma”. Every other name follows from that one. The verse also gives his two parents, Añjanā and the Wind, matching Vālmīki.',
 'Atulita means unweighable, beyond comparison: in the Sundara Kāṇḍa no rākṣasa, not even Rāvaṇa’s son Akṣa, can match him. But the verse puts the title of messenger first, before strength. A messenger has no agenda of his own; his greatness lies in carrying another’s word faithfully. That is the Cālīsā’s picture of Hanumān: a mighty being whose identity is service.',
 'Whatever your strength, ask whose message you carry. The answer gives the strength its point.',
 'First <b>Rāma’s messenger</b>, then the strong one.'),
X(5,'THE HERO WITH LIMBS OF THUNDER',HS('Hanuman with a mace driving away a dark cloud','BAJARAṄGĪ','storm'),
 'Great hero, mighty in valour, with limbs as hard as the thunderbolt: you drive away bad thinking and are the companion of good sense.',
 [('mahābīra bikrama','<b>great hero, of great valour</b>'),('bajaraṅgī','<b>with limbs like the vajra</b>'),('kumati nivāra','<b>remover of bad thoughts</b>'),('sumati ke saṅgī','<b>companion of good thoughts</b>')],
 'Bajaraṅgī (vajrāṅgī) gave rise to the popular name Bajrangbalī, “the strong one with thunderbolt limbs”. It recalls Indra’s vajra that struck the infant Hanumān and could not harm him, and the boon that it never would.',
 'The second line turns from outer to inner battle. Kumati, a twisted mind, is the real enemy for most of us; sumati, good understanding, is the friend. Tulasīdāsa says elsewhere in the Rāmacaritamānas that where sumati lives, prosperity follows, and where kumati lives, disaster. Hanumān’s strength is thus pointed inwards: the same force that crushed rākṣasas can crush our worst impulses.',
 'Notice the bad thought before you act on it. That pause is where Hanumān’s help begins.',
 'He drives off <b>bad thinking</b>.'),
X(6,'GOLDEN AND RADIANT',HS('Hanuman in golden colour with earrings and curly hair','KAÑCANA BARANA','crown'),
 'Your colour is golden, you shine in fine clothes, with earrings in your ears and curly hair.',
 [('kañcana barana','<b>golden in colour</b>'),('birāja subesā','<b>shining, well dressed</b>'),('kānana kuṇḍala','<b>earrings in the ears</b>'),('kuñcita kesā','<b>curly hair</b>')],
 'Here begins a short dhyāna, a description to hold in the mind while chanting. Many hymns have one: before worship, the devotee pictures the deity in detail. This verse and the next give Hanumān’s form.',
 'The golden colour matches Vālmīki, who often compares Hanumān to gold, to the rising sun, or to a mountain of gold such as Meru when he grows huge. Sindūr has made most temple images orange, but the texts see him as golden-tawny. Earrings were a mark of nobility and learning; curly hair, a mark of beauty. The picture is of a prince, not a wild animal, which matches the Rāmāyaṇa’s view of the vānaras as a noble people.',
 'Picture what you pray to. A clear image steadies the mind.',
 'A <b>golden</b> prince of the forest.'),
X(7,'MACE, FLAG AND SACRED THREAD',HS('Hanuman holding a thunderbolt-mace and a flag, wearing a grass thread','VAJRA · DHVAJĀ','book'),
 'In your hand shine the thunderbolt and the flag; on your shoulder you wear the sacred thread of muñja grass.',
 [('hātha bajra au dhvajā','<b>thunderbolt (or mace) and flag in hand</b>'),('birājai','<b>shine</b>'),('kāṃdhe mūṃja janeū','<b>muñja-grass sacred thread on the shoulder</b>'),('sājai','<b>adorns</b>')],
 'Bajra here is usually read as the mace or a thunderbolt-like weapon, and the flag as a banner of victory, perhaps Rāma’s own. In many North Indian shrines Hanumān holds both, and saffron flags fly over every Hanumān temple.',
 'The muñja-grass thread is a detail that tells a lot. The janeū, sacred thread, marks a student who has been initiated into Vedic learning; muñja grass is the material prescribed for the girdle of a brahmacārī in the Gṛhya Sūtras. So the verse quietly calls Hanumān a lifelong celibate student, a scholar as well as a warrior. Some ask how a vānara wears a sacred thread; the devotional answer is that learning and devotion, not birth, make the twice-born.',
 'Carry both: the weapon for the fight and the flag for what you fight for.',
 'Warrior’s <b>mace</b>, student’s <b>thread</b>.'),
X(8,'ŚIVA’S OWN, KESARĪ’S JOY',HS('Hanuman with a trident and crescent moon faintly behind him','ŚAṄKARA SUVANA','moon'),
 'Portion of Śiva, joy of Kesarī: your brilliance and glory are great, and the whole world bows to you.',
 [('saṅkara suvana','<b>son (or portion) of Śiva</b>'),('kesarīnandana','<b>delight of Kesarī</b>'),('teja pratāpa mahā','<b>great radiance and power</b>'),('jaga bandana','<b>worshipped by the world</b>')],
 'Some editions read “saṅkara svayaṃ”, Śiva himself, instead of “saṅkara suvana”, Śiva’s son. Either way the line brings in the Śaiva tradition that Hanumān is Śiva’s portion, born to serve Rāma, and puts it beside his family name: joy of Kesarī.',
 'Tulasīdāsa was a great bridge-builder between Śaivas and Vaiṣṇavas. In his Rāmacaritamānas, Śiva tells the Rāma story to Pārvatī, and Rāma worships Śiva at Rāmeśvaram. By calling Hanumān Śiva’s own, he makes Hanumān the place where both traditions meet: Śiva serving Viṣṇu. Teja is inner fire or brilliance; pratāpa is the power that makes others bow; bandana (vandana) means the salutation of all the world.',
 'Great people often belong to more than one family. Let them.',
 '<b>Śiva’s own</b>, in Rāma’s service.'),
X(9,'LEARNED, GOOD AND QUICK',HS('Hanuman with a book, eager to go','RĀMA KĀJA','book'),
 'Learned, full of virtues and very clever, you are always eager to do Rāma’s work.',
 [('bidyāvāna','<b>learned</b>'),('gunī','<b>virtuous</b>'),('ati cātura','<b>extremely clever</b>'),('rāma kāja karibe ko ātura','<b>eager to do Rāma’s work</b>')],
 'This verse moves from his form to his character. It echoes Vālmīki’s Kiṣkindhā Kāṇḍa, where Rāma marvels at Hanumān’s learning after hearing him speak once.',
 'Ātura is a strong word: impatient, almost restless. Hanumān does not wait to be told twice; in the Sundara Kāṇḍa he refuses even to rest on the mountain Maināka. Cātura, clever, is shown again and again: his choice of ordinary speech for Sītā, his trick with Surasā, his decision to let Indrajit bind him so he could see Rāvaṇa. Learning, goodness and cleverness are all put to one use, Rāma’s work. Devotees say that is what turns talent into virtue.',
 'Use your gifts for something worth doing, and do it without being asked twice.',
 'Always <b>eager</b> for Rāma’s work.'),
X(10,'IN LOVE WITH THE LORD’S STORY',HS('Hanuman listening intently to a recitation, Rama, Lakshmana and Sita in his heart','RASIYĀ','heart'),
 'You delight in hearing the Lord’s story; Rāma, Lakṣmaṇa and Sītā live in your mind.',
 [('prabhu caritra','<b>the Lord’s deeds</b>'),('sunibe ko rasiyā','<b>a lover of hearing</b>'),('rāma lakhana sītā','<b>Rāma, Lakṣmaṇa, Sītā</b>'),('mana basiyā','<b>dwell in the mind</b>')],
 'This verse is the root of the belief that Hanumān is present wherever the Rāmāyaṇa is told. A rasiyā is a connoisseur, someone who savours: Hanumān never tires of hearing what he himself witnessed.',
 'Hearing (śravaṇa) is the first of the nine forms of devotion listed in the Bhāgavata Purāṇa, and Hanumān is often named its great example. The second line anticipates the later story of the torn chest: Rāma, Lakṣmaṇa and Sītā live inside him. Tulasīdāsa’s own life fits the verse: by tradition he found Hanumān at a Rāmāyaṇa reading in Vārāṇasī, the first to come and the last to leave.',
 'What you love to hear about, you become. Choose your stories.',
 'He never tires of <b>Rāma’s story</b>.'),
X(11,'TINY FOR SĪTĀ, HUGE FOR LAṄKĀ',HS('A tiny Hanuman before Sita and a giant one over burning Lanka','SŪKṢMA · BIKAṬA','fire'),
 'Taking a tiny form, you showed yourself to Sītā; taking a terrible form, you burned Laṅkā.',
 [('sūkṣma rūpa dhari','<b>taking a subtle, tiny form</b>'),('siyahiṃ dikhāvā','<b>you appeared to Sītā</b>'),('bikaṭa rūpa dhari','<b>taking a fearsome form</b>'),('laṅka jarāvā','<b>you burned Laṅkā</b>')],
 'Now the hymn turns to his deeds, retelling the Sundara Kāṇḍa in two lines. Both forms come from Vālmīki: Hanumān appears small to Sītā so as not to frighten her, and grows vast when he burns the city.',
 'The pairing is the point. The same being who can shrink to a whisper for a frightened woman can grow terrible for a city of tyrants. Power that knows when to be small is the rarest kind. Commentators also read sūkṣma as subtle in the spiritual sense: to reach Sītā, who stands for the soul or for Śakti, one must become subtle and humble; to burn the city of ego, one must be fierce.',
 'Be gentle with the frightened and firm with the bully. Know which moment is which.',
 '<b>Small</b> for Sītā, <b>huge</b> for Laṅkā.'),
X(12,'THE TERRIBLE FORM',HS('Hanuman as a giant, striking demons','BHĪMA RŪPA','demon'),
 'Taking a terrible form, you destroyed the asuras and accomplished Rāmacandra’s work.',
 [('bhīma rūpa dhari','<b>taking a terrifying form</b>'),('asura saṃhāre','<b>you destroyed the demons</b>'),('rāmacandra ke kāja','<b>Rāmacandra’s tasks</b>'),('saṃvāre','<b>you set right, accomplished</b>')],
 'This line covers the war in the Yuddha Kāṇḍa: Hanumān kills Dhūmrākṣa, Akampana, Devāntaka, Triśiras and Nikumbha, and fights Rāvaṇa himself. Bhīma, terrible, is also the name of his “brother” in the Mahābhārata, the other son of the Wind.',
 'Saṃvāre means more than “did”: it is to arrange, set right, make beautiful. Hanumān does not just fight; he puts Rāma’s affairs in order. The asuras of the verse are read by many teachers as inner enemies as well: lust, anger, greed, delusion, pride and envy, the six foes of the mind. Rāmacandra, “Rāma the moon”, is the gentle name, set against the terrible form: fierceness in service of gentleness.',
 'Sometimes doing the right thing needs a strong hand. Make sure the work is not your own ego’s.',
 'Fierce, for <b>Rāma’s</b> work.'),
X(13,'THE HERB THAT SAVED LAKṢMAṆA',HS('Hanuman bringing the mountain of herbs, Rama embracing him','SAÑJĪVANĪ','mountain'),
 'Bringing the life-giving herb, you revived Lakṣmaṇa; Rāma, hero of the Raghus, joyfully held you to his heart.',
 [('lāya sajīvana','<b>bringing the sañjīvanī herb</b>'),('lakhana jiyāye','<b>you revived Lakṣmaṇa</b>'),('śrīraghubīra haraṣi','<b>Rāma, delighted</b>'),('ura lāye','<b>embraced you to his chest</b>')],
 'The most famous episode of Hanumān in the war: Lakṣmaṇa struck down, the physician Suṣeṇa naming the herb, and Hanumān bringing the whole mountain from the Himalaya. In Tulasīdāsa’s Laṅkā Kāṇḍa the night is full of drama, with the false sage Kālanemi and Bharata’s arrow.',
 'Tulasīdāsa’s Rāma, waiting through the night beside his brother, laments as a human brother would: he could face losing a wife, but not Lakṣmaṇa. When Hanumān arrives before dawn, the embrace is not a king’s reward but a brother’s relief. Ura lāye, “brought to the chest”, is an intimate phrase. This is the second of Rāma’s embraces; the first was after Sītā was found.',
 'When someone you love is in danger, do more than asked. Bring the mountain.',
 'He brought <b>life</b> back to Lakṣmaṇa.'),
X(14,'DEAR AS BHARATA',HS('Rama praising Hanuman, Hanuman bowing','BHARATAHI SAMA',rama=True),
 'Rāma praised you greatly: “You are as dear to me as my brother Bharata.”',
 [('raghupati kīnhī','<b>the Lord of the Raghus did</b>'),('bahuta baḍāī','<b>great praise</b>'),('tuma mama priya','<b>you are dear to me</b>'),('bharatahi sama bhāī','<b>like brother Bharata</b>')],
 'This and the next verse report Rāma’s words. In the Rāmacaritamānas, after Lakṣmaṇa is revived, Rāma calls Hanumān a brother, and in Vālmīki too Rāma repeatedly says he can never repay Hanumān.',
 'Why Bharata and not Lakṣmaṇa? Bharata is the brother who loved Rāma from a distance: he refused the throne, ruled from a village as Rāma’s deputy, and slept on the ground for fourteen years. His love was service without reward, exactly like Hanumān’s. To be called “equal to Bharata” is the highest praise in Tulasīdāsa’s world, where Bharata is often called the greatest devotee of all.',
 'Love that serves without asking is the kind that is called family.',
 'As <b>dear as Bharata</b>.'),
X(15,'A THOUSAND MOUTHS SING YOU',HS('Rama embracing Hanuman, a many-headed serpent singing behind','SAHASA BADANA',rama=True),
 '“The thousand-mouthed one sings your glory,” said the Lord of Śrī, and embraced you.',
 [('sahasa badana','<b>the thousand-mouthed (Śeṣa)</b>'),('tumharo jasa gāvaiṃ','<b>sing your fame</b>'),('asa kahi śrīpati','<b>so saying, Lakṣmī’s lord</b>'),('kaṇṭha lagāvaiṃ','<b>embraced you</b>')],
 'Sahasa badana, the thousand-mouthed, is Ādiśeṣa, the cosmic serpent on whom Viṣṇu rests, whose thousand mouths praise the Lord without end. Lakṣmaṇa is held to be Śeṣa’s incarnation, so there is a quiet pun: the brother Hanumān saved will sing his praise forever.',
 'Śrīpati, “Lakṣmī’s husband”, is a Viṣṇu name: Tulasīdāsa reminds us that the prince embracing his vānara servant is the Lord of the universe. Kaṇṭha lagāvaiṃ, “held to the throat”, is the warmest form of embrace in Indian idiom. In the theology of bhakti this is the summit: not liberation, not power, but the Lord’s own embrace.',
 'Praise people warmly, and in public. It costs little and lasts a lifetime.',
 'The <b>thousand-mouthed</b> serpent sings him.'),
X(16,'ALL THE GODS AND SAGES',HS('Sages and gods singing in a row','MUNĪSA','gods'),
 'Sanaka and the sages, Brahmā and the gods, the great munis, Nārada, Sarasvatī, and the king of serpents too…',
 [('sanakādika','<b>Sanaka and his brothers</b>'),('brahmādi munīsā','<b>Brahmā and the great sages</b>'),('nārada sārada','<b>Nārada, Śāradā (Sarasvatī)</b>'),('sahita ahīsā','<b>with the lord of serpents</b>')],
 'Verses 16 and 17 form one sentence: all these beings, the next verse says, cannot fully describe Hanumān’s glory. This kind of list is common in hymns: the greater the singers who fail, the greater the subject.',
 'The four Kumāras, Sanaka, Sanandana, Sanātana and Sanatkumāra, are Brahmā’s mind-born sons who remain eternally children, absorbed in knowledge. Nārada is the wandering sage-musician who carries news between worlds; Śāradā is Sarasvatī, goddess of speech, who should be the best poet of all. Ahīśa is again Śeṣa. To list them is to say that knowledge, music, speech and endless praise all fall short.',
 'Some things are bigger than words. Notice them anyway.',
 'Sages, gods and <b>Sarasvatī</b> herself.'),
X(17,'NO POET CAN TELL IT',HS('Guardians of the directions and poets falling silent','KAHĀṂ TE','star'),
 '…Yama, Kubera and the guardians of the directions: when even they cannot tell your glory, how could poets and scholars?',
 [('jama kubera','<b>Yama, Kubera</b>'),('digapāla jahāṃ te','<b>the direction-guardians, when even they</b>'),('kabi kobida','<b>poets and learned men</b>'),('kahi sake kahāṃ te','<b>how could they describe?</b>')],
 'Yama rules death and the south; Kubera, wealth and the north; the dikpālas guard all eight directions. These were among the gods who gave the infant Hanumān boons after the Wind’s anger. Now they cannot find words for what that child became.',
 'This is Tulasīdāsa’s modest bow at the centre of the hymn: if gods fail, a poet surely will. It is a classic Indian convention, but meant sincerely: Tulasīdāsa calls himself a servant at the end of the Cālīsā, not an author. Kobida (kovida) means a learned expert. The verse also protects the hymn from pride: whatever is said here is only a fragment.',
 'Say what you can, and admit it is not all.',
 'No poet can tell it <b>all</b>.'),
X(18,'YOU MADE SUGRĪVA KING',HS('Hanuman bringing Rama to Sugriva, a crown between them','RĀJA PADA','crown'),
 'You did Sugrīva a great favour: you brought him to Rāma and gave him the royal throne.',
 [('tuma upakāra','<b>you did a kindness</b>'),('sugrīvahiṃ kīnhā','<b>to Sugrīva</b>'),('rāma milāya','<b>uniting him with Rāma</b>'),('rāja pada dīnhā','<b>gave him kingship</b>')],
 'Back to the story: the Kiṣkindhā Kāṇḍa. Hanumān, sent by the frightened Sugrīva, met Rāma, brought him to Sugrīva, and arranged their alliance by fire. Vālī died, and Sugrīva became king.',
 'Notice where the credit goes: it was Rāma who killed Vālī, but the verse says Hanumān gave Sugrīva the throne, because he made the meeting happen. In devotional reading, this is the guru’s role: the teacher does not save you himself; he introduces you to the Lord who does. Hanumān is often called the great introducer, bringing others to Rāma: Sugrīva, Vibhīṣaṇa, and Tulasīdāsa.',
 'Be the one who introduces people who need each other.',
 'He brought <b>Sugrīva</b> to Rāma.'),
X(19,'VIBHĪṢAṆA TOOK YOUR ADVICE',HS('Hanuman advising Vibhishana, who later wears a crown','MANTRA','crown'),
 'Vibhīṣaṇa followed your counsel and became lord of Laṅkā, as all the world knows.',
 [('tumharo mantra','<b>your counsel</b>'),('bibhīṣana mānā','<b>Vibhīṣaṇa accepted</b>'),('laṅkeśvara bhae','<b>he became lord of Laṅkā</b>'),('saba jaga jānā','<b>the whole world knows</b>')],
 'In Tulasīdāsa’s Sundara Kāṇḍa, Hanumān meets Vibhīṣaṇa in Laṅkā while searching for Sītā; Vibhīṣaṇa is chanting Rāma’s name, and they become friends. Hanumān tells him of Rāma’s grace. This meeting is not in Vālmīki, but it shapes the northern tradition.',
 'Mantra here means counsel, advice. Hanumān’s advice was simple: go to Rāma; he never rejects one who seeks refuge. Vibhīṣaṇa followed it and was crowned on the seashore before the war even began. In Vālmīki, Hanumān also speaks for accepting Vibhīṣaṇa in Rāma’s war council. Twice, then, he makes a king: first Sugrīva, then Vibhīṣaṇa. Both were younger brothers wronged by elder brothers, and both found refuge through him.',
 'Good advice given at the right moment can change a life. Give it gently.',
 'His <b>advice</b> made a king.'),
X(20,'THE SUN AS A SWEET FRUIT',HS('Baby Hanuman leaping at the sun','MADHURA PHALA','sun',mace=False),
 'The sun, thousands of yojanas away, you swallowed, taking it for a sweet fruit.',
 [('juga sahasra jojana','<b>a yuga-thousand yojanas</b>'),('para bhānū','<b>the sun, far away</b>'),('līlyo tāhi','<b>you swallowed it</b>'),('madhura phala jānū','<b>thinking it a sweet fruit</b>')],
 'The hymn goes back to his boyhood, told in Vālmīki’s Uttara Kāṇḍa: the hungry baby leapt for the rising sun, was struck by Indra, and was given boons by all the gods. Tulasīdāsa says “swallowed”, as the folk tradition does; Vālmīki has him only leap for it.',
 'This line has become famous for a modern reason. Some read “yuga sahasra yojana” as numbers to multiply: a yuga as 12,000, a sahasra as 1,000, a yojana as about 8 miles, which gives roughly 96 million miles, close to the earth–sun distance of about 93 million. Others point out that the units and values are disputed and that the line is poetry. We note the reading without endorsing it; the verse’s own point is the child’s innocence and power.',
 'Children reach for the impossible because no one has told them it is. Keep a little of that.',
 'He took the <b>sun</b> for a fruit.'),
X(21,'THE RING IN HIS MOUTH',HS('Hanuman leaping over the ocean, a ring held in his mouth','MUDRIKĀ','ocean'),
 'Holding the Lord’s ring in your mouth, you leapt across the ocean: no wonder in that.',
 [('prabhu mudrikā','<b>the Lord’s signet ring</b>'),('meli mukha māhīṃ','<b>placing it in your mouth</b>'),('jaladhi lāṃghi gaye','<b>you crossed the ocean</b>'),('acaraja nāhīṃ','<b>no surprise</b>')],
 'Rāma gave his ring to Hanumān alone, in Kiṣkindhā, to show Sītā. Tulasīdāsa adds the lovely detail of carrying it in his mouth for safety on the leap.',
 'Why “no wonder”? Because one who carries Rāma’s name can cross anything. The ring bore Rāma’s name, and a popular teaching says that it was the name, not Hanumān’s strength, that carried him across. Hanumān himself says so in some tellings: the vānaras only built the bridge; the stones floated because of the name. The ocean of the verse is also read as the ocean of saṃsāra, the cycle of birth and death, crossed by holding the Lord’s name close.',
 'Hold on to what matters most. Then the crossing is less frightening.',
 'With Rāma’s <b>ring</b>, the ocean was easy.'),
X(22,'HARD THINGS MADE EASY',HS('A steep path becoming smooth before a devotee','SUGAMA','mountain'),
 'Whatever tasks in the world are hard, by your grace they all become easy.',
 [('durgama kāja','<b>difficult tasks</b>'),('jagata ke jete','<b>as many as are in the world</b>'),('sugama anugraha','<b>easy by grace</b>'),('tumhare tete','<b>your grace, that many</b>')],
 'The hymn now turns from Hanumān’s past deeds to what he does for devotees. Durgama, “hard to go”, and sugama, “easy to go”, are a simple pair; jete…tete is “as many…so many”.',
 'Indian students recite this verse before examinations, and travellers before hard journeys. Its logic follows from the story: one who made the ocean crossable can make your path crossable too. Anugraha, grace, is the key word: not the devotee’s effort alone, and not magic, but help freely given. Teachers add a caution: grace makes the task easy, not unnecessary; Hanumān still had to leap.',
 'Pray for help, then start walking. Grace meets you on the road.',
 'By his <b>grace</b>, the hard becomes easy.'),
]
